The charge of an electron is approximately \(1.60 \times 10^{-19}\) C. To find the number of excess electrons that carry a total charge of \(-1\) C, we divide the total charge by the charge of one electron:

\[
\text{Number of electrons} = \frac{-1\ \text{C}}{-1.60 \times 10^{-19}\ \text{C}}
= \frac{1}{1.60 \times 10^{-19}}
= 6.25 \times 10^{18}
\]

So, the object contains \(6.25 \times 10^{18}\) excess electrons.

Answer: A
